# IPv6 counterpart of ../iptables/rules-save. ICMPv6 must pass broadly —
# neighbour discovery and PMTU die without it, unlike ICMPv4.
#
# SSH IS SCOPED HERE AND NOT IN THE IPv4 FILE, on purpose. IPv4 hosts sit
# behind NAT, so port 22 is only reachable if someone deliberately forwards it.
# IPv6 has no NAT: a machine with a global address is reachable from the whole
# internet on every open port, and the only thing in the way is whatever the
# ISP's router happens to filter. An unscoped "-p tcp --dport 22 ACCEPT" on
# IPv6 therefore publishes sshd to the world — that is the single most obvious
# exposure these images had (found on nvhard 2026-08-13, global 2a02:… address).
#
# The scope is deliberately layered so a prefix change cannot lock anyone out:
#   fe80::/10  link-local. NEVER changes, works from any machine on the same
#              segment ('ssh user@fe80::…%eth0'). This is the safety net, and
#              it is why narrowing the GUA below is not a lockout risk.
#   fd00::/8   ULA, for a site that runs its own stable prefix.
# A site-specific global prefix is NOT baked into the image — it would be
# wrong on every machine but one. Add it per host, e.g.
#   ip6tables -I INPUT 4 -s <prefix>::/64 -p tcp --dport 22 \
#       -m conntrack --ctstate NEW -j ACCEPT
# and mirror it into this file on that host. If the ISP rotates the prefix the
# rule stops matching: IPv4 on the LAN and IPv6 link-local both still work, so
# the failure mode is "GUA ssh quietly stops", not "no way in".
*filter
:INPUT DROP [0:0]
:FORWARD DROP [0:0]
:OUTPUT ACCEPT [0:0]
-A INPUT -i lo -j ACCEPT
-A INPUT -m conntrack --ctstate ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT
-A INPUT -p ipv6-icmp -j ACCEPT
# Same brute-force brake as the IPv4 file, and it matters more here: IPv6 has
# no NAT in front of it. Separate bucket name — v4 and v6 keep independent
# hashtables (/proc/net/ipt_hashlimit vs ip6t_hashlimit), and one name per
# table keeps them readable. Must come BEFORE the ACCEPTs below to have any
# effect. REQUIRES CONFIG_NETFILTER_XT_MATCH_HASHLIMIT (live.fragment).
-A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -m conntrack --ctstate NEW -m hashlimit --hashlimit-name ssh6 --hashlimit-mode srcip --hashlimit-above 15/minute --hashlimit-burst 30 -j DROP
-A INPUT -s fe80::/10 -p tcp --dport 22 -m conntrack --ctstate NEW -j ACCEPT
-A INPUT -s fd00::/8 -p tcp --dport 22 -m conntrack --ctstate NEW -j ACCEPT
# mDNS/DNS-SD (printer discovery, 2026-08-14) — multicast ff02::fb replies
# never match ESTABLISHED; dport-only for symmetry with the v4 rule.
-A INPUT -p udp --dport 5353 -j ACCEPT
COMMIT
